    terms: [["Stewardship", "Managing what God has entrusted to us."], ["Household plan", "A shared set of family guidelines for technology."], ["Discernment", "Judging wisely between good and harmful."]],
    intro: "We have spent twelve weeks studying how to guide children through the digital world. We saw that technology is a tool that needs wise use, that parents should see clearly both opportunities and risks, that the heart is the deeper issue, and that parents' own habits teach powerfully. We built a household plan, protected rest, and considered guidance for little children, older children and teens. We reflected on tools and supervision, social media and identity, and entertainment. Today we gather these lessons and prepare for the test.",
    context: "This is the midpoint of the course and the default week for the mid-session test, which covers lessons 1 to 12. Churches may hold the test during class time or on another day. Where it is on another day, this session serves as a guided review. 1 Corinthians 6:12 and Proverbs 4:23 sum up the first half: use freedom wisely without being mastered, and guard the heart from which life flows.",
    observe: "The first half has laid the foundation for a biblical approach to technology in the family: wisdom, heart, example, planning, rest and age-appropriate guidance. The second half will address cyberbullying, pornography, open conversation, predators and privacy, misinformation, faith online, digital fasting, repair when things go wrong, different family situations, discipling the digital generation and future technologies.",
    points: [
      ["A biblical stance toward technology", "Technology is a tool; we test whether it is helpful and whether it masters us, we see risks and opportunities clearly, and we aim at God's glory. Recall 1 Corinthians 6:12, 10:31, Matthew 10:16 and Proverbs 14:15. Ask learners to think of one example from daily life and share it with a neighbour."],
      ["Heart, example and household plan", "The deeper issue is the heart, parents model wise use, and a clear shared plan brings order and fairness. Recall Proverbs 4:23, Matthew 15:19, Psalm 90:12, Proverbs 24:3-4 and Luke 14:28. Invite learners to say how this truth could shape one decision this week."],
      ["Rest, ages and tools", "Rest and sleep are protected, guidance changes with age, tools support but do not replace presence, and identity and entertainment are weighed by Scripture. Recall Psalm 127:2, Mark 10:16, Psalm 119:9, Proverbs 27:23, 1 Samuel 16:7 and Philippians 4:8."],
    ],
    discuss: [
      ["Which lesson has most changed how you think about technology in your home?", "Ask each learner to name one lesson and one change, and tie their answers to the passages studied."],
      ["How would you explain a biblical view of technology to a friend who feels overwhelmed?", "Invite short, hopeful explanations built on wisdom and a Bible verse."],
      ["Which habit from the first half have you started or want to start?", "Encourage a concrete step with a day, a time and a person to help."],
      ["What questions about children and technology do you still have?", "Record them so they can be answered in the second half of the course or with your pastor."],
    ],
    apply: "Write a one-page summary of the first twelve lessons with a Scripture reference for each key idea. Review it twice before the test. Share one lesson with another parent and thank God for each in prayer.",
    activity: "Play a team review game. The teacher reads a clue such as be wise as serpents and innocent as doves and teams call out the topic and a passage. Give extra points for application. End by reading 1 Corinthians 6:12 and Proverbs 4:23 together.",
    takeHome: "Prepare for the test by reviewing the revision map and memory verses. Write five practice questions with answers and test someone.",
    prayer: "Thank God for wisdom to guide our children. Ask for calm minds for the test. Pray that what we have learned will protect and bless the next generation.",
    teacher: "Explain how the mid-session test works: time, number of questions and when results are released. Reassure anxious learners. If the test is held on another date, use this session as a review. Close with prayer and encouragement.",
    assess: "This unit is the mid-session test week. The mid-session test covers Lessons 1 to 12 and is built from the approved question bank following the curriculum blueprint.",
    mcq1: ["Which verse says keep your heart with all vigilance?", ["Proverbs 4:23", "Genesis 1:1", "Psalm 23", "Acts 2"], 0, "Proverbs 4:23."],
    mcq2: ["Which verse says all things are lawful but not all are helpful?", ["1 Corinthians 6:12", "John 3:16", "Romans 3:23", "Hebrews 11:1"], 0, "1 Corinthians 6:12."],
    tf: ["The mid-session test covers lessons 1 to 12 of this course.", true, "It reviews the first half of the course."],
    sa: ["Summarise in two or three sentences how Christian parents should approach technology.", ["wisdom", "heart", "example", "plan", "rest", "love"], "A good answer says parents treat technology as a tool to be used wisely for God's glory, look to the heart, model good habits, set a clear household plan, protect rest and guide children according to age with love."],
